// KNOWN FAILURE: parsing a NESTED binary expression corrupts a LATER tokenize.
//
// Expected: "after parse: 31 tokens"
// Actual:   "after parse: 1 tokens"
//
// Parsing `fn f() { return x + y * 2 }` and then tokenizing an UNRELATED
// string returns 1 token instead of 31. With deallocation disabled
// (KRYOS_FREE_DIAG=1) it instead dies inside the lexer with
//
//   kryos_array_push: corrupt array header (len=0, cap=4,
//                     elem_size=2478245608176, ref_count=2)
//
// `elem_size` holding a POINTER means the header is being read at the wrong
// offset or written over -- and because it reproduces with nothing being
// freed, this is NOT a use-after-free.
//
// BISECTED (each step run; this is the whole trail so nobody repeats it):
//
//   two tokenizes back to back                     ok
//   tokenize + parser_new                          ok
//   + expect / cur_text / advance / alloc_node     ok  (each alone)
//   + parse_expr / parse_stmt / parse_block        ok  (each alone)
//   parse_fn on `fn f() {}`                        ok
//   parse_fn on `fn f() { return 1 }`              ok
//   parse_fn on `fn f(x: i64) { return 1 }`        ok  (params alone)
//   parse_fn on `fn f() { return x + y }`          ok  (one binop)
//   parse_fn on `fn f() -> i64 { return x + y }`   ok  (return type)
//   parse_fn on `fn f() { return x + y * 2 }`      BREAKS   <- nested binop
//
// So the trigger is parse_expr RECURSING through precedence climbing
// (`parse_expr(pp, prec + 1)` inside its own loop), not params, not the
// return type, not a single operator.
//
// NOT YET REPRODUCED standalone. Two hand-built models of that exact shape --
// a recursive precedence climber threading a struct and pushing into its
// array fields, once with 3 fields and once matching the real Parser's 7
// array fields -- are both CLEAN. So something else about the real program is
// load-bearing; find it before theorising about the cause.
//
// This is the last known corruption in stage1_mini_parser.kry and the only
// thing keeping selfhost-stage1 red.
//
// THE RUNNABLE REPRO LIVES AT compiler/self-host/known_failure_nested_binop.kry
// -- it needs the self-host module search path (`use token`, `use lexer`), so
// it cannot run from this directory. Run it with:
//
//     cd compiler/self-host && ../target/release/kryos.exe run known_failure_nested_binop.kry
//
// This file is the write-up; that file is the executable case.
